text:
ethan.design: Dropping the Night Owl theme explorations — focusing on SQL/code panes and timeseries chart readability on dark backgrounds. Figma: https://www.figma.com/file/night-owl-explore Please quick-react.
claire.a11y: Skimming now. First shout: inline SQL snippets at 13px feel low-contrast for screen reader users who increase magnification. We should aim for >=4.5:1 for body-level inline text.
oscar.frontend: Noted. We want to avoid extra DOM for inline pills if possible. Canvas charts are the bigger perf concern — halos hurt frame rates on heavy dashboards.
ethan.design: Current working tokens (draft):
```css
--bg-deep: #071018;
--panel-surface: #0c1418;
--text-muted: #9aa7ad;
--text-main: #e8f6f8;
--code-bg: #06131a;
--code-keyword: #7fd7ff;
--code-string: #ffd6a7;
--code-fn: #d9b6ff;
--accent-green: #28b79f;
--accent-red: #ff6f5f;
--accent-gold: #f2bb00;
```
marco.eng: From runtime: if charts use alpha blends we should pick accents with higher raw luminance delta so they remain readable when overlaid. Also prefer using 1-pixel thicker strokes rather than halos for perf-safe separation.
nina.data: I'll run a quick CVD + grayscale sim but early note: green/red combos need careful lightness spacing. I can try green -> #00b39f and red -> #ff5544 to maximize separability.
claire.a11y: Automated scan on tokens: text-muted on panel-surface = 3.2:1 (fails). code-keyword on code-bg = 5.1:1 (ok for blocks, marginal inline). Recommend bumping text-muted to ~#cbdfe4 or adding inline pill for code.
oscar.frontend: Bumping text token is one CSS change, pill is renderer work. I'm +1 for token change as quick win. Also, for charts we can enable dashed pattern fallback for narrow widths (no extra compositing) — the canvas strokeDash approach is cheap.
ethan.design: Two proposals: (A) raise --text-muted to #cbdfe4 and update accents to nina's picks; (B) keep muted but add inline pill + dashed/outline fallback for charts. Which is less friction?
marco.eng: Option A is lower risk and quicker to roll. Option B gives nicer visuals but blocks on renderer work. I'd pick A for this sprint.
sam.pm: Copy thought: label the setting as "Theme: Dark / Light" and put a secondary control "High contrast (improves legibility)" under Accessibility. Users search for "dark mode" though, so include that term in docs.
ethan.design: Agreed on copy. I'll update the figma tokens: --text-muted -> #cbdfe4, --accent-green -> #00b39f, --accent-red -> #ff5544, keep gold -> #f2bb00 and add --focus-ring: #ffd86b.
nina.data: Posted quick simulation: with those accents, separability in protan/deutan/tritan modes > 90% for common series counts. Grayscale mapping keeps at least 12 lightness steps between series.
claire.a11y: Extra request: add an ARIA description for the theme toggle explaining what "High contrast" does and include a small checklist in the PR: contrast spots, keyboard focus, inline code under 14px, and chart legend readability with screen reader labels for series. I'll draft it.
oscar.frontend: Implementation note: if we merge token changes, please include a small CSS snippet in the PR that maps the tokens to our chart lib colors so we can validate before the deploy.
ethan.design: Will include token CSS + short changelog. Plan: push token PR by EOD, mark as "fast-follow" for inline-pill renderer work next sprint if needed. If no objections I'll merge tomorrow morning. :rocket:
prodops-bot: Release note draft created: "Design: Night Owl dark tokens updated to improve legibility and chart separability (see PR #)."
marco.eng: I'll run a quick perf smoke on the metrics cluster after token rollout and report any regressions.
claire.a11y: Thanks — I'll attach the accessibility checklist to the PR and run manual spot tests on a 13" MBP and NVDA + macOS voiceover.
ethan.design: Thanks everyone — appreciate the quick feedback. I'll tag folks in the PR when it's up.
